        "content": "<p>Hi everyone! Just wanted to give a quick update on the status of Riemann-Roch because I have recently achieved (what I think is) a nice milestone. If X is locally noetherian integral scheme of krull dimension at most one which is locally finite over a field k such that the cohomology groups of O_X eventually vanish and are finite dimensional k-vector spaces, then we have the usual Euler characteristic form of Riemann-Roch for curves <code>χ(𝒪ₓ(D)) = deg(D) + χ(𝒪ₓ)</code>. These cohomological assumptions on O_X are equivalent to X being proper over k, which we do not show here (and indeed this is quite nontrivial to show). </p>\n<p>This work builds upon work in mathlib PRs by Brian Nugent (<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/36218\">#36218</a>) and Jesse Alama (<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/29713\">#29713</a>). You can have a look at this stuff in my draft PR <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41621\">#41621</a>, though be warned the code is still quite messy (I'll be cleaning things up and upstreaming to Mathlib over the next little while). </p>\n<p>The main result is riemann_roch in RiemannRoch.lean,</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">riemann_roch</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Scheme</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Field</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Over</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Spec</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">CommRingCat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">of</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"o\">))]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsIntegral</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsNoetherian</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsRegularInCodimensionOne</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">D</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">AlgebraicCycle</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℤ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">KrullDimLE</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↥</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LocallyOfFiniteType</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Spec</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">CommRingCat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">of</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"o\">))]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hχ₀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Modules</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">HasFiniteEulerCharacteristic</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">structureSheafModule</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hD</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsWeilDivisor</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">D</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">sheaf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">D</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">eulerChar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">degree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">D</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">structureSheafModule</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">eulerChar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 609621041,
        "sender_full_name": "Raphael Douglas Giles",
        "timestamp": 1783788657
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"673690\">Raphael Douglas Giles</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/116395-maths/topic/Progress.20towards.20Riemann-Roch/near/609621041\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>These cohomological assumptions on O_X are equivalent to X being proper over k, which we do not show here (and indeed this is quite nontrivial to show).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Although this may be hard, we only need that properness implies finiteness in practice, not the other way around. Is that any easier?</p>",
